WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:06.940
Given a matrix, how do we find its adjoint?

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:11.740
This is a two-by-two matrix 'A', and we need to find its adjoint.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:15.420
This is a new term and maybe slightly confusing.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:20.100
But it will be easy, if you concentrate well for the next two minutes.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:24.740
To find the adjoint we first need to find some matrix.

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:26.920
What is this some matrix?

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:30.800
It will be of the same order as the original matrix.

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:34.000
So we need to find the four elements here,

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:37.700
Each element will be the cofactor of the corresponding element

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:39.880
from the original matrix.

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:43.980
So here we will have the cofactor of the one oneth element.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:49.040
Here, we will have the cofactor of the one twoeth element and so on...

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:50.620
Once this matrix is done,

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:55.960
we need to find its transpose and that will give us the adjoint of "A".

00:00:56.360 --> 00:01:01.680
We find the cofactors first and then find the transpose of this matrix.

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:03.680
The first element is two.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:06.040
What will be its cofactor?

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:11.620
It will be 'negative 1' raised to '1 plus 1' multiplied by its minor.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:16.760
1 and 1 are the row number and column number of 2.

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:18.540
What will be its minor?

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:20.700
We exclude the row and the column,

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:23.820
and find the determinant of what remains.

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:28.680
That will be the Minor. The determinant of 3 will be 3.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.740
So this would be multiplied with 3, this is the cofactor of 2.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.700
'Negative 1' raised to 2 is 'positive 1',

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:40.840
and 1 multiplied with 3 will equal 3.

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.820
3 is the cofactor of 2.

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:48.200
Next we find the cofactors of 5 ,1 and 3 .

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:51.380
The element 5 lies in the one twoeth position.

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:56.160
So we multiply 'negative 1' raised to '1 plus 2' with its minor.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:58.000
To find the minor of 5.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:00.420
We exclude row 1 and column 2

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:03.640
and find the determinant of what remains..

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:06.280
The determinant of 1 is 1.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:12.740
1 is the minor of 5. So we multiply this with 1 to get the cofactor.

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:16.180
Negative 1 times 1, will give us negative 1.

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:19.380
Negative one is the cofactor of five.

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:23.940
Now pause the video and try to find the cofactors of one and three.

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:34.480
We get the cofactor of one as 'minus 5'. And that of three as two.

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:37.320
These are the four cofactors we found.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:39.660
Now we could write this matrix down,

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:44.140
three, minus one, minus five and two.

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:47.020
We have to find the transpose of this matrix.

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:52.020
So we mark a slant line here ! This will be the adjoint of 'A'.

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:55.280
Do you remember how to find the transpose of a matrix?

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:58.440
Yes, we interchange the rows and the columns.

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:01.180
The first row will become the first column,

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:05.500
3, minus 1 and the second row will become the second column,

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:07.740
minus 5, 2.

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:10.420
That's the adjoint of matrix 'A'.

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:12.320
Find the cofactors first,

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:15.820
and then find the transpose of the resulting matrix.

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:20.000
We will understand a few more examples in the next part of the session.

